one:
External1: &ext1;
External2: &ext2;
External3: &ext3;
Internal1: &int1;
Internal2: &int2;
Internal3: &int3;

Defined in DTD: &definedInDtd;
Defined in external DTD: &definedInDtd1;
Referenced in content: %internal;


Output:
External1: external1
External2: in two: external1
External3: in three: %externalValue1;
Internal1: internal1
Internal2: internal2: internal1
Internal3: %internal3;

Defined in DTD: I was defined in the DTD
Defined in external DTD: I was #1 defined in the DTD
Referenced in content: %internal;

Conclusions:
- The differences in parsing between the the value-internal processing is significant when something is used in the DTD, but not in the non-dtd,
   because the processor will not try to interpret an PE ref in the content.
- So, my interpretation is that reading an external entity will parse parameter entities and char refs.
   As these come out, of course the PE refs are replaced by their referenced text.  So we get charref, text, charref text
   If this is delivered to the content, (e.g. &ext3; and &int3; here) the char refs are treated as % which are just inlined as regular text.
      (just as you can put %foo; in the text and it will be ignored)
- What is more interesting is the value-internal entities.  in these cases, the character references become characters at the poitn of processing.
   - again, in content this is ignored (&int3). However, %dtdItem; expands completely fine, since in the DTD the character is escaped and so happy to be used.
      in contrast, dtdItemExt2 produces an error because what comes out is a charref, which we can't use here.

So, the processing model is this:
   On encounter:
      value-internal: replace charrefs and prefefs
      value-external: nothing
   On use
      value-internal: process with charrefs acting as characters
      value-external: process


   xmllint --loaddtd  --noent  --valid 32-internalExternalRef.xml
